<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Isly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-moroccan-war">Franco-Moroccan War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Isly was fought on 14 August 1844 near the Isly river, close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oujda">Oujda</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, between a French army under Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-robert-bugeaud">Thomas-Robert Bugeaud</a> and a much larger Moroccan force led by the sultan's son. The French victory ended Morocco's support for Abd al-Qadir's resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and exposed the weakness of the Moroccan army, opening the country to growing European pressure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-isly.jpg" alt="Battle of Isly" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vernet_-_Bataille_d%27Isly_-_1846.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-algiers">French invasion of Algiers</a> in 1830, Abd al-Qadir led the tribes of the Mascara region against the French from 1832. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tafna">Treaty of Tafna</a> of 1837, signed by Bugeaud, recognised his authority over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in return for his recognition of French sovereignty, but in November 1839, supported by Sultan Abd al-Rahman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, he declared war again. Bugeaud, governor from 1840, undertook a systematic conquest, and after the Battle of the Smala in 1843 Abd al-Qadir was driven into the desert and took refuge in Morocco.</p><p>Border fighting followed in 1844. On 30 May a Moroccan force attacked the French camp at Lalla Maghnia and was repulsed by General Lamoricière; on 19 June French troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oujda">Oujda</a>, and on 6 August French ships under François d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, a son of the French king, bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>. Bugeaud brought on the battle without a declaration of war, to force the sultan to give up his support for Abd al-Qadir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Bugeaud marched 11,000 men on the Moroccan camp at Djarf el-Akhdar, on the right bank of the Isly a short distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oujda">Oujda</a>. Expecting to face cavalry, he formed his infantry into a great diamond of small squares with the cavalry inside. At daybreak 20,000 to 25,000 Moroccan horsemen charged but could not break the lines and were split by the advancing squares. French cavalry then attacked in echelons: six squadrons of spahis under Colonel Joseph took a Moroccan camp and its eleven guns before the gunners could reload, while a second echelon under Colonel Morris engaged the cavalry on the right, and the Moroccan infantry scattered into ravines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French took eleven guns, eighteen flags and all the Moroccan tents, among them the commander's tent and umbrella, the equivalent of a captured standard. About 800 Moroccans and 27 French were killed, with 99 French wounded. The bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essaouira">Mogador</a> began the next day, and Bugeaud was made Duke of Isly. Morocco sued for peace and declared Abd al-Qadir a mufsid, a corrupter, rather than a holy warrior; relations between them worsened into open conflict over the border regions, and after three more years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare Abd al-Qadir surrendered to Lamoricière in 1847.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Isly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1844 Bugeaud defeated a Moroccan army supporting Abdelkader at the <b>Battle of Isly</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeats by France at <b>Isly</b> in 1844 and by Spain in 1860 opened Morocco to European pressure, and the Treaty of Fez of 1912 created the French protectorate in Morocco; a Franco-Spanish treaty later that year set up the Spanish protectorate in Morocco in the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Morocco's defeat at <b>Isly</b> in 1844 cost him support, and he surrendered in 1847.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-abdelkader">Emirate of Abdelkader</a></li></ul></section>
